{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 11,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.104,
            0.476,
            0.121
          ],
          "content": "PAIR OF LINEAR EQUATIONS IN TWO VARIABLES"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.873,
            0.105,
            0.899,
            0.119
          ],
          "content": "35"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.139,
            0.901,
            0.178
          ],
          "content": "In the example above, we eliminated y. We could also have eliminated x. Try doing it that way."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.122,
            0.182,
            0.9,
            0.221
          ],
          "content": "2. You could also have used the substitution, or graphical method, to solve this problem. Try doing so, and see which method is more convenient."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.226,
            0.734,
            0.244
          ],
          "content": "Let us now note down these steps in the elimination method :"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.253,
            0.901,
            0.292
          ],
          "content": "**Step 1 :** First multiply both the equations by some suitable non-zero constants to make the coefficients of one variable (either x or y) numerically equal."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.296,
            0.9,
            0.335
          ],
          "content": "**Step 2 :** Then add or subtract one equation from the other so that one variable gets eliminated. If you get an equation in one variable, go to Step 3."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.339,
            0.901,
            0.378
          ],
          "content": "If in Step 2, we obtain a true statement involving no variable, then the original pair of equations has infinitely many solutions."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.382,
            0.9,
            0.421
          ],
          "content": "If in Step 2, we obtain a false statement involving no variable, then the original pair of equations has no solution, i.e., it is inconsistent."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.425,
            0.848,
            0.445
          ],
          "content": "**Step 3 :** Solve the equation in one variable (x or y) so obtained to get its value."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.448,
            0.901,
            0.487
          ],
          "content": "**Step 4 :** Substitute this value of x (or y) in either of the original equations to get the value of the other variable."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.492,
            0.624,
            0.511
          ],
          "content": "Now to illustrate it, we shall solve few more examples."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.524,
            0.901,
            0.563
          ],
          "content": "**Example 9 :** Use elimination method to find all possible solutions of the following pair of linear equations :"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.392,
            0.567,
            0.897,
            0.586
          ],
          "content": "2x + 3y = 8\\tag{1}",
          "image_source": "images/page_11_equation_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.392,
            0.59,
            0.897,
            0.609
          ],
          "content": "4x + 6y = 7\\tag{2}",
          "image_source": "images/page_11_equation_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.614,
            0.205,
            0.633
          ],
          "content": "**Solution :**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.648,
            0.901,
            0.687
          ],
          "content": "**Step 1 :** Multiply Equation (1) by 2 and Equation (2) by 1 to make the coefficients of x equal. Then we get the equations as :"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.391,
            0.692,
            0.897,
            0.71
          ],
          "content": "4x + 6y = 16\\tag{3}",
          "image_source": "images/page_11_equation_17.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.392,
            0.715,
            0.897,
            0.734
          ],
          "content": "4x + 6y = 7\\tag{4}",
          "image_source": "images/page_11_equation_18.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.739,
            0.599,
            0.758
          ],
          "content": "**Step 2 :** Subtracting Equation (4) from Equation (3),"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.264,
            0.763,
            0.561,
            0.781
          ],
          "content": "(4x - 4x) + (6y - 6y) = 16 - 7",
          "image_source": "images/page_11_equation_20.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.787,
            0.14,
            0.805
          ],
          "content": "i.e.,"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.451,
            0.786,
            0.772,
            0.805
          ],
          "content": "0 = 9, which is a false statement."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.812,
            0.556,
            0.831
          ],
          "content": "Therefore, the pair of equations has no solution."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.844,
            0.902,
            0.902
          ],
          "content": "**Example 10 :** The sum of a two-digit number and the number obtained by reversing the digits is 66. If the digits of the number differ by 2, find the number. How many such numbers are there?"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.435,
            0.966,
            0.563,
            0.983
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        }
      ]
    }
